---
// src/pages/blog/panty-fit-guide.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['panty-fit-guide'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Panties Are Where Fit Goes Wrong: A Manufacturing Guide"
  description="Rise, gusset construction, leg elastic and fabric recovery — the four production decisions that decide whether a panty fits, rides up, or gets returned."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Panty Fit</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-panty-fit-hero.webp" alt="Lace panty front view showing rise and leg opening construction" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">November 8, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Panties Are Where Fit Goes Wrong: A Manufacturing Guide</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Bra development gets all the attention and all the budget. Panties get treated as the easy
        companion piece — a few panels of fabric, an elastic waistband, done. In our experience the
        return rates say the opposite. A bra that is slightly off gets worn anyway; a panty that rides
        up, digs in at the leg or shows through becomes an immediate return and a one-star review.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four production decisions cause almost all of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Why panties fail more often than bras</h2>
      <p>
        The reason is structural rather than a matter of attention. A bra has adjustable variables -
        straps, hook positions, band tension - that absorb fit error across a range of bodies. A panty
        has almost none. It either sits where the wearer expects or it does not, and there is no second
        chance once it is on the body.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why the four decisions below are made on the tech pack rather than in the fitting room.
        By the time a first sample exists, the outcome is largely settled.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Rise is a specification, not a style label</h2>
      <p>
        "Low rise", "mid rise" and "high rise" are treated as marketing language, but they are production
        measurements and they need to be specified in centimetres on the pattern. The measurement that
        matters is the front rise — from the crotch seam to the top of the waistband at centre front.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure mode is subtle: a rise that is 2cm too short sits on the hip instead of over it, and
        the product photographs identically. It only shows up when worn, which is when it becomes a
        return. Since the customer cannot see the difference before buying, this is the single easiest
        place to lose trust.
      </p>
      <p>
        Practical rule: if you are producing one silhouette across a size range, the rise increment per
        size step matters as much as the waist measurement. Under-grading the rise is very common and is
        why larger sizes so often feel like they are cut for a smaller frame.
      </p>

      <h2>2. The gusset is the quality signal</h2>
      <p>
        The gusset — the lined panel at the base — is where a customer judges whether a panty is worth
        its price, because it is the part they feel. Three specifications decide it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lining fabric.</strong> A cotton or cotton-rich lining is the expectation. A synthetic lining reads as cheap instantly and is a genuine comfort issue.</li>
        <li><strong>Stitch construction.</strong> A flatlock or coverstitch over a raw overlock edge. The overlock edge on a gusset is a common cost cut and a common complaint.</li>
        <li><strong>Attachment method.</strong> Fully enclosed (clean-finished inside) versus overlocked in place. Enclosed costs more per unit in labour and is worth it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is a component where we never advise cost-down. It is the part of the garment in contact
        with the customer, and it is the part they will inspect.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8">
        <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark">
          <Picture src="blog-panty-fit-detail.webp" alt="Black and red lace panty showing leg opening elastic finishing" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-xs text-slate mt-2">Leg opening elastic tension and finishing decide whether a panty stays put.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>3. Leg elastic tension is the ride-up culprit</h2>
      <p>
        Riding up is almost never a sizing problem — it is leg elastic tension. Too loose and the fabric
        migrates upward with movement; too tight and it digs into the crease at the leg. The window is
        narrower than most people assume, and it has to be specified as a tension at a given extension
        rather than as "soft elastic".
      </p>
      <p>
        Two production factors move it. First, elastic quality: a cheap elastic loses recovery within
        tens of washes and the leg opening relaxes — so a panty that was correct in QC becomes a panty
        that rides up in month two. Second, application tension: the same elastic sewn at different
        tensions gives different fits, which is why panty leg elastic should be specified with a target
        stretched length rather than left to the operator's feel.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Fabric recovery, especially on seamless</h2>
      <p>
        On a seamless or bonded panty, there is no elastic at the leg — the fabric itself is the elastic.
        That makes the fabric specification the entire fit specification. Two-way stretch with high
        recovery (elastane in the 15–20% range) behaves completely differently from a basic knit at the
        same price point after ten washes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard test is simple and worth insisting on: cut a fixed length, stretch it to a fixed
        extension, hold, release, and measure permanent deformation after repeated cycles. Fabric that
        grows more than a few percent permanently in that test will grow in the customer's drawer.
      </p>

      <h2>The one-piece vs cut-and-sew decision</h2>
      <p>
        This is the structural choice that frames everything above. A seamless circular-knit panty is
        cheaper per unit at volume and has no side seams to feel, but the fit is locked into the knit and
        cannot be adjusted per style — you change the fit by changing the machine programme, which is a
        tooling cost. A cut-and-sew panty costs more in labour but gives you per-style control over rise,
        gusset and leg opening, and lets you run small runs of many styles.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a core basic line at volume, seamless is the efficient answer. For a fashion line with
        frequent style turnover, cut-and-sew is the only practical option. Trying to build a
        fast-turnover fashion line on seamless machines is a mismatch that usually surfaces as an
        inability to react to trends quickly enough.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the cost actually sits</h2>
      <p>
        Buyers are often surprised that a panty and a bra can land within a dollar of each other. The
        breakdown explains it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fabric</strong> - the largest line on a basic panty, and the one most exposed to weight and recovery grade. This is where a cheap quote is cheap, and where it shows after the third wash.</li>
        <li><strong>Labour</strong> - proportional to panel count less than you would expect, but highly sensitive to gusset finishing, which is fiddly and often hand-guided.</li>
        <li><strong>Leg elastic</strong> - small per unit, disproportionate in effect. The grade decides returns more than any other component on the garment.</li>
        <li><strong>Trims</strong> - a bow, a charm, a printed care label: each one is a separate SKU with its own minimum order quantity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A panty quoted 30% below your other quotes is usually cheaper on fabric weight or on gusset
        finishing. Both are invisible in a photograph and obvious in wear.
      </p>

      <h2>The wash test you should run before bulk</h2>
      <p>
        Fit cannot be judged from a first-wear sample. Run three wash cycles at 40 degrees, tumble dry,
        then measure again: waistband height, front rise, leg opening circumference and overall length.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Length change under 2%</strong> - acceptable for cotton-rich jersey.</li>
        <li><strong>Leg opening growth over 5%</strong> - the elastic grade is wrong and the panty will ride up.</li>
        <li><strong>Any visible pilling at the gusset seam</strong> - the lining fibre or the stitch is wrong, and it will not improve.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This test costs three sample units and one afternoon. It catches the failure modes that produce
        one-star reviews, and it is the single highest-return activity in panty development.
      </p>

      <h2>What to put in the spec sheet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Front rise and back rise in centimetres, per size</li>
        <li>Gusset lining fabric composition and attachment method</li>
        <li>Leg elastic: type, width, target stretched length at application</li>
        <li>Fabric stretch and recovery grade, with wash-cycle recovery tolerance</li>
        <li>Waistband elastic recovery tolerance</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this is exotic. It is simply the difference between specifying a panty and ordering one.
        The brands with the lowest returns in this category almost always turn out to have a spec sheet
        with numbers on it rather than adjectives.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Producing a panty line?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">We spec rise, gusset and leg elastic numerically on every style, and run recovery tests before bulk. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Send your spec</a> or see our <a href="/products/panties" class="text-accent hover:underline">panty range</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <BlogSchema slug="panty-fit-guide" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
